      "id": "amttjei3qfb4",
      "title": "Ultrasound localisation microscopy reveals mural neovascularisation to diagnose and track giant cell arteritis",
      "titleEn": "",
      "catId": "cmtsfnrexstk",
      "year": "2025",
      "journal": "Annals of the Rheumatic Diseases",
      "authors": "The First Hospital of China Medical University",
      "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ard.2025.10.017",
      "link": "https://ard.eular.org/article/S0003-4967(25)04456-5/abstract",
      "tags": [],
      "abstract": "Giant cell arteritis (GCA) is an inflammatory blood vessel disease in older adults. Conventional ultrasound can’t see the abnormal new microvessels inside the wall. \n\nThis study use URM in GCA patients, revealing dense, twisted microvascular networks within the thickened arterial wall. After treatment, these microvessels largely disappeared — visible on URM before wall thickness normalized. \n\nURM directly visualizes the inflammatory microvessels instead, enabling real-time, non-invasive assessment of disease activity and treatment response.",
      "findings": [
        "Baseline URM detected abundant mural neovascularisation in the superficial temporal artery, with quantitative metrics: vessel ratio 11.32%, mean density 5.47, mean velocity 10.05 mm/s and perfusion index 1.88.",
        "After 4-month glucocorticoid therapy, all four parameters markedly declined to 0.59%, 2.45, 5.68 mm/s and 0.09, accompanied by obvious regression of intramural microvessels.",
        "No mural microvascular signals were seen in healthy controls."
      ],
      "cover": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
      "gallery": [],
      "galleryLayout": "auto",
      "featured": false,
      "visible": true
